window.STUDY_INDEX = [{"kind": "Topic", "title": "The memory model", "text": "A C++ program does not get one undifferentiated bag of memory. The process owns an address space, and the language puts code, globals, call frames, and new blocks into different regions of it. Every later picture in this course — a pointer on the stack, a buffer on the heap, a by-value argument — is a drawing of those regions. Java, C#, and Python hide the same machine behind a garbage collector (איסוף זבל). C++ gives the programmer the keys: new is taking the key to an apartment, delete is handing it back. Forget to hand it back, and the apartment stays rented. The five regions Chapter 1 draws the address space from high addresses down to low addresses. The names in the slides are the ones to keep. Process address space A running process has its own range of addresses. Inside that range the course separates five regions: region Hebrew name size who frees it access stack מחסנית grows and shrinks automatic, LIFO read and write heap ערימה grows and shrinks the programmer, with delete read and write uninitialized statics משתנים לא מאותחלים fixed at compile time the process lifetime read and write initialized statics משתנים מאותחלים fixed at compile time the process lifetime read and w", "href": "topics/memory-model.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "Student ps = new Student(\"Avi\"); runs inside main. Say where ps lives, where the Student l", "text": "Student ps = new Student(\"Avi\"); runs inside main. Say where ps lives, where the Student lives, and what remains after main returns if nobody calls delete.", "href": "topics/memory-model.html#worked-stack-array"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Practical session 2 draws the same split. For each line, say whether the named storage is ", "text": "Practical session 2 draws the same split. For each line, say whether the named storage is on the stack or the heap, and what delete is required. void demo() {\n    int local = 2;\n    int* one = new int(2);\n    int* many = new int[10];\n    string* s = new string(\"I'm on the heap\");\n}", "href": "topics/memory-model.html#ps2-stack-vs-heap"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2015 moed A, question 2 (30 points). A vector stores elements in a contiguous array and ca", "text": "2015 moed A, question 2 (30 points). A vector stores elements in a contiguous array and can resize. The course decides that is wasteful, and asks for mvector\u003cT>, whose memory is exactly the cells it needs, stored as a linked list. a. (15) Implement the functions. You may add helpers. You may not add fields or change signatures. insertAt always succeeds, allocating if it must, and inserts at pos. removeAt shrinks the structure and, if pos is empty, returns some dummy T. Access to a linked-list ce", "href": "topics/memory-model.html#exam-15a-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2015 moed B, question 2 (30 points). mvector is now an array of node, not a list. insertAt", "text": "2015 moed B, question 2 (30 points). mvector is now an array of node, not a list. insertAt at a pos past the end grows the array to the smallest power of two strictly larger than pos (an empty vector, insert at 5, becomes length 8). removeAt shrinks by half once every element in the upper half has been removed (length 8, after the four high elements are gone, becomes 4). Elements keep their indexes. node has a pure virtual duplicate(). a. (15) Implement the underlined operations, including a dee", "href": "topics/memory-model.html#exam-15b-q2"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Pointers and references", "text": "A pointer (מצביע) is a variable whose value is the address of another cell. The machine does not know the name s. It knows \"read 2 bytes from address 4\" or \"write 4 bytes at address 8, and treat them as a signed int.\" The type on the pointer is what tells the compiler how many bytes that means, and how to scale arithmetic. RAM in this lecture is a row of bytes. Each byte is 8 bits. Each byte has an index, starting at 0, and that index is the address. On the course machines a pointer itself occupies 8 bytes, because the system is 64-bit. What a pointer is Pointer A pointer is a variable that stores an address. Through it the program can read or write at that address and after it. The declaration short ps says that ps points at a short. The address-of operator & produces the address of an object. The dereference operator reads or writes the object at the stored address. short s = 17978;\nshort* ps;          // declaration\nps = &s;            // assignment\nshort* ps2 = &s;    // initialization\n*ps = 3;            // dereference, write\nstd::cout \u003c\u003c *ps;   // dereference, read The compiler's table for a variable has three columns: name, type, address. At run time the CPU only sees the ad", "href": "topics/pointers.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "int i = 2; int pi = &i; int ppi = &pi; What do i, pi, ppi, and ppi print, and what does &i", "text": "int i = 2; int pi = &i; int ppi = &pi; What do i, pi, ppi, and ppi print, and what does &i equal?", "href": "topics/pointers.html#worked-pointer-trace"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "For each fragment from the practical session, name the fault. Do not \"fix\" it by deleting ", "text": "For each fragment from the practical session, name the fault. Do not \"fix\" it by deleting a line that the session says still compiles. int* p = nullptr;\n*p = 3;\n\nint* q;\n*q = 3;\n\nint* r = new int;\ndelete r;\n*r = 5;\n\nint* a = new int;\nint* b = a;\ndelete b;\n*a = 3;", "href": "topics/pointers.html#ps2-pointer-dangers"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "This is the session's two-dimensional allocation. Free every block it obtained. Do not fre", "text": "This is the session's two-dimensional allocation. Free every block it obtained. Do not free a row twice. int** arry = new int*[rowCount];\nfor (int i = 0; i \u003c rowCount; ++i)\n    arry[i] = new int[colCount];", "href": "topics/pointers.html#ps2-delete-2d"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2020 moed A, question 1, Memory (25 points). Write a C++ program that stores two kinds of ", "text": "2020 moed A, question 1, Memory (25 points). Write a C++ program that stores two kinds of image, Portrait and Scenery. Each pixel is a gray level in 0..255. Pixels of one image are kept in a one-dimensional BYTES array, row after row. An ImageRepository holds many images in an array named _images. a. (10) Implement the constructor and Insert. The constructor allocates an array of capacity pointers. Insert receives an array of images, deep-copies that pointer array, and does not deep-copy the byt", "href": "topics/pointers.html#exam-20a-q1"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Classes and objects", "text": "A C++ class is a layout plus functions that receive a hidden pointer to that layout. The practical sessions start one step earlier, because a class is useless until the compiler and the linker have turned two files into one program. This topic is that path: how a program is built, how an object is constructed, and how a call sees the object. Building a program A C++ function does not have to live inside a class. A name declared outside every class is in the global scope. A definition gives the prototype and the body. A declaration gives only the prototype and a semicolon. You may declare a function many times and define it once. A forward declaration tells the compiler the shape of a name whose body comes later. Names needed by more than one .cpp file go in a header, and each user #includes that header. #include pastes the file in. Include headers, not other .cpp files, and guard the header so a cycle does not paste it twice: #ifndef POINT_H\n#define POINT_H\n// declarations\n#endif The build has four stages, from practical session 1. Compile and link The preprocessor takes a .cpp file, strips comments, and obeys # directives (#include, #ifndef, #define). The compiler turns that into ", "href": "topics/classes-and-objects.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "Point has const int x, y. Why does the constructor need : x(x), y(y), and why does a Line ", "text": "Point has const int x, y. Why does the constructor need : x(x), y(y), and why does a Line that wants len computed in the initializer call a static function?", "href": "topics/classes-and-objects.html#worked-initializer"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 1's comparison table asks where linking happens. State, for Java and for C++, when", "text": "Session 1's comparison table asks where linking happens. State, for Java and for C++, when unresolved calls are tied to their definitions, and what file g++ -c produces.", "href": "topics/classes-and-objects.html#ps1-link-vs-java"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "A makefile lists clean first and hello second. hello depends on helloWorld.o and run.o. Yo", "text": "A makefile lists clean first and hello second. hello depends on helloWorld.o and run.o. You type make in that directory. What runs, and what would you type to build hello?", "href": "topics/classes-and-objects.html#ps1-makefile-first-target"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "emphasize should append three exclamation marks to the caller's string. The session shows ", "text": "emphasize should append three exclamation marks to the caller's string. The session shows three signatures. Which ones change the caller's string, and which one can be given a null? void emphasize(std::string word);\nvoid emphasize(std::string& word);\nvoid emphasize(std::string* word);", "href": "topics/classes-and-objects.html#ps3-pass-by-value"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Rule of three", "text": "If a class owns a resource, a memberwise copy is a lie. The lecture's Image owns a char _bytes allocated in the constructor. Copying the object copies the pointer. Both objects then believe they own one buffer, and the first destructor frees it out from under the second. The three operations Rule of three If a class needs a user-written destructor, a user-written copy constructor, or a user-written copy-assignment operator, it almost certainly needs all three. The destructor (דיסטרקטור) frees the resource. The copy constructor (בנאי העתקה) allocates a new resource and copies the contents into it. The assignment operator (אופרטור השמה) does both: it frees what this object already owns, and it takes a deep copy of the other object. Image a(...);\nImage b = a;   // copy constructor, not assignment\nb = a;         // assignment, both objects already exist The destructor has an empty parameter list and no return type. The lecture writes it ~Image() and calls delete[] _bytes. In Java, dropping the last reference to an Image would also make the byte array unreachable. In C++, destroying the Image object destroys the pointer, not the bytes, unless the destructor says so. Shallow copy, then t", "href": "topics/rule-of-three.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "avi holds a 1080 by 1920 buffer A. benni holds buffer B. Trace avi = benni implemented as ", "text": "avi holds a 1080 by 1920 buffer A. benni holds buffer B. Trace avi = benni implemented as copy-and-swap, and say which buffer is freed.", "href": "topics/rule-of-three.html#worked-copy-and-swap"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 3 quotes the rule as: if a class needs a user-defined destructor, copy constructor", "text": "Session 3 quotes the rule as: if a class needs a user-defined destructor, copy constructor, or copy-assignment operator, it almost certainly needs all three. A List holds Node head allocated with new. The compiler's generated special members are still in place. What breaks, and which three functions do you write?", "href": "topics/rule-of-three.html#ps3-rule-of-three"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2017 moed A, question 2 (25 points). Students in \"C-Kalkel\" store test results in C++. Tes", "text": "2017 moed A, question 2 (25 points). Students in \"C-Kalkel\" store test results in C++. TestRes holds a char description_. Test manages a collection of those results. The question asks you to make the classes correct under copy and destruction: the description buffer is owned by the result, and a copied test must not share that buffer. Implement the special members the ownership requires. Do not leak the previous description on assignment.", "href": "topics/rule-of-three.html#exam-17a-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2017 moed B, question 2 (25 points). Terrain\u003cT> stores a board as a T block of height_ wid", "text": "2017 moed B, question 2 (25 points). Terrain\u003cT> stores a board as a T block of height_ width_ cells. Implement the two constructors and the destructor. The copy constructor must be a deep copy. (8 points on the copy.) template \u003ctypename T>\nclass Terrain {\npublic:\n    Terrain(int w, int h);\n    Terrain(const Terrain& other);\n    ~Terrain();\n};", "href": "topics/rule-of-three.html#exam-17b-q2"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Rule of five and zero", "text": "A copy is the honest way to give two objects the same picture. It is the expensive way to return a picture from a function. C++11 adds a second channel: a temporary can be gutted. The resource moves, the bytes do not. Values that have an address, and values that do not lvalue and rvalue An lvalue (ערך מיקום, historically a left-hand value) is an object with an address you can take, assign through, and bind a reference to. An rvalue (ערך זמני) is a temporary. It can appear on the right of an assignment, and it is about to disappear: the result of a computation, or an object returned from a function. A const lvalue reference binds to both. It extends the temporary's life to the reference's scope and refuses modification. std::move(x) does not move anything. It is a cast that lets the compiler treat the lvalue x as an rvalue, so an overload that steals resources becomes legal. After a real move, x must still be a valid object. The lecture's moved-from Image holds nullptr and must be safe to destroy. An rvalue reference is written &&. It binds to a temporary. It is the parameter type of a move constructor and a move-assignment operator. void convert(const Image& img);   // binds to a n", "href": "topics/rule-of-five.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "Image benny(std::move(avi)); where avi owns a pixel buffer. What does benny._bytes hold, w", "text": "Image benny(std::move(avi)); where avi owns a pixel buffer. What does benny._bytes hold, what does avi._bytes hold, and what does ~Image on avi free?", "href": "topics/rule-of-five.html#worked-move-image"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 4 asks how many times the copy constructor runs for a function that takes MyClass ", "text": "Session 4 asks how many times the copy constructor runs for a function that takes MyClass by value and returns MyClass by value, and what changes when the parameter becomes const MyClass&. Assume no elision, so you can count the copies the language rules insert. Then say which of those copies NRVO is allowed to remove.", "href": "topics/rule-of-five.html#ps4-copy-count"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "The session's closing examples ask you to predict two programs. sp2 is a shared_ptr copied", "text": "The session's closing examples ask you to predict two programs. sp2 is a shared_ptr copied from sp1, then sp2 is destroyed. up2 is a unique_ptr initialized from up1 with copy initialization. What happens in each case?", "href": "topics/rule-of-five.html#ps4-unique-vs-shared"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2016 moed B, question 2 (30 points). ITEM_COLLECTION\u003cT> stores T items. byVal takes the co", "text": "2016 moed B, question 2 (30 points). ITEM_COLLECTION\u003cT> stores T items. byVal takes the collection by value. a. (10) Write a deep copy constructor. Mind the template syntax. b. (5) Complete a main so the collection can hold both SPHERE and BOX, which derive from SHAPE. c. (5) The student switches to the default copy constructor. What breaks in byVal(myShapeCollection)? d. (10) Repair the ownership with shared_ptr, without changing the collection's job. shared_ptr is the class from the lecture.", "href": "topics/rule-of-five.html#exam-16b-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2020 moed B, question 1, Memory (25 points). The question is the smart-pointer pattern fro", "text": "2020 moed B, question 1, Memory (25 points). The question is the smart-pointer pattern from class. Efi is implementing SmartPointer\u003cT> with a T ptr_, and parts of the class were erased. Complete it as the lecture's shared_ptr: a reference count, copy that shares the object, destruction that frees only at count zero, and no double free. Helpers are allowed. (15 points for the class.)", "href": "topics/rule-of-five.html#exam-20b-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2024 moed A, question 1, Memory (35 points). A Java BinaryTree\u003cT extends Comparable\u003cT>> st", "text": "2024 moed A, question 1, Memory (35 points). A Java BinaryTree\u003cT extends Comparable\u003cT>> stores data, left, and right, and insert walks left when compareTo is positive and right otherwise, ignoring duplicates. compareTo returns 1 when this is greater than other, and -1 when it is smaller. Assume T supports the operators you need. a. (10) Write the basic C++ class: fields, the two constructors, getters, and insert. b. (15) Complete the class under the rule of five. c. (10) Draw the memory at the c", "href": "topics/rule-of-five.html#exam-24a-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2024 moed C, question 1, Memory (35 points). Copy-on-write: a clone shares the object unti", "text": "2024 moed C, question 1, Memory (35 points). Copy-on-write: a clone shares the object until a write happens. Only the first modification allocates a private copy. a. Complete copy_on_write\u003cT>. It holds T ptr. T has clone(). on_write() is called before the first assignment through this wrapper, and that is when the clone must happen if the object is still shared. b. Adjust the fragment so the optimization actually applies, and draw memory at the marked points. A* a = new A(3);\nA* b = a->clone(); ", "href": "topics/rule-of-five.html#exam-24c-q1"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Inheritance and polymorphism", "text": "Inheritance (ירושה) builds a new class on an existing one. The base class (מחלקת בסיס) is the one that already exists. The derived class (מחלקה נגזרת) adds members and can replace behavior. The colon in the class head is the C++ spelling of Java's extends. class BlurFilter : public Filter {\n    double _str;\npublic:\n    BlurFilter(double strength) : Filter(\"blur\"), _str(strength) {}\n    double getStrength() { return _str; }\n}; The derived constructor must call a base constructor. If it does not, the compiler tries the base default constructor, and the program fails to compile when the base has none. Session 3 shows both the explicit Student(...) : Person(name) {} and the implicit form that only compiles when Person can be built with no arguments. What is-a actually requires An upcast (המרה למעלה) stores a derived object in a base pointer or a base reference. The justification on the slide is the is-a relation: a BlurFilter is a Filter. void process(Image& img, const Filter& filter);\nBlurFilter blur(50.0);\nprocess(img, blur); The same upcast works with unique_ptr\u003cFilter> holding a BlurFilter. Liskov substitution The Liskov substitution principle (עקרון ההחלפה של ליסקוב): a derived ob", "href": "topics/inheritance.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "Filter filter = new BlurFilter(); filter->apply(...); with apply virtual. Which function r", "text": "Filter filter = new BlurFilter(); filter->apply(...); with apply virtual. Which function runs, and what changes if you remove virtual?", "href": "topics/inheritance.html#worked-virtual-call"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 3: a derived object is deleted through a base pointer, and the destructor is not v", "text": "Session 3: a derived object is deleted through a base pointer, and the destructor is not virtual. What is the result, and what is the rule the session states for a class that is meant to be a base?", "href": "topics/inheritance.html#ps3-virtual-destructor"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2016 moed A, question 2 (30 points). A game engine has SHAPEs (SPHERE, BOX) drawn by SHADE", "text": "2016 moed A, question 2 (30 points). A game engine has SHAPEs (SPHERE, BOX) drawn by SHADERs (BASIC_SHADER, COMPLEX_SHADER). Each shader has Render(SPHERE) and Render(BOX). RENDER_MGR::Render loops over shapes and shaders. Overload resolution on a SHAPE cannot pick Render(SPHERE). a. (20) Implement the visitor pattern so the loop dispatches to the right pair. SHAPE and SHADER must be abstract. Add code only in the classes named in the question. b. (10) Write a deep copy constructor for RENDER_MG", "href": "topics/inheritance.html#exam-16a-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2018 moed A, question 1, memory management (30 points). Image is an abstract base with int", "text": "2018 moed A, question 1, memory management (30 points). Image is an abstract base with int n_pixels and virtual void show() = 0. ColorImage derives from it and owns a pixel array. The multiple-choice claims include: the program leaks because ~ColorImage never runs; the fix is virtual ~Image() {}; ColorImage also needs its own destructor that delete[]s the array; there is no leak. Choose the right claim, and say what a by-value copy of a ColorImage does to the array under the rule of three.", "href": "topics/inheritance.html#exam-18a-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2018 moed B, question 1 (30 points). The same image hierarchy, rewritten so black-and-whit", "text": "2018 moed B, question 1 (30 points). The same image hierarchy, rewritten so black-and-white and color images share one abstract base and differ in the pixel type. Draw the stack and the heap at the comment inside show, including vptrs. A later part asks which cast finds the dynamic type. The official discussion includes dynamic_cast.", "href": "topics/inheritance.html#exam-18b-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2019 moed A, question 1 (30 points). APP is abstract with virtual void run() = 0. PAY_APP ", "text": "2019 moed A, question 1 (30 points). APP is abstract with virtual void run() = 0. PAY_APP and PLAY_APP override it. MY_APPS_REPO must store both kinds in one array. a. (5) Give apps_repo_arr_ a single type that can hold both. b. (5) Implement MY_APPS_REPO(int n_pay_app, int n_play_app) to allocate and store that many of each. c. (10) The class learned a way to stop clients calling new on the repository, by hiding construction. Implement that, and show how a client still obtains an instance. d. (", "href": "topics/inheritance.html#exam-19a-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2019 moed B, question 1 (30 points). TESTER declares three overloads, test(APP), test(PAY_", "text": "2019 moed B, question 1 (30 points). TESTER declares three overloads, test(APP), test(PAY_APP), test(PLAY_APP), all virtual in the sense of the base, but a call tester->test(app) where app has static type APP binds the APP overload at compile time. Overload resolution does not use the dynamic type. Draw the memory for the run up to the comment (10 points), and explain why a PAY_APP object still hits test(APP).", "href": "topics/inheritance.html#exam-19b-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2024 moed B, question 1, Memory (35 points). BinaryTree\u003cT> has left and right. InorderBina", "text": "2024 moed B, question 1, Memory (35 points). BinaryTree\u003cT> has left and right. InorderBinaryTree derives from it and also implements a traversal interface. Draw memory at the end of: Traversable\u003cBinaryTree\u003cint>>* t1 = new InorderBinaryTree\u003cBinaryTree\u003cint>>(\n    BinaryTree\u003cint>(2),\n    new InorderBinaryTree\u003cBinaryTree\u003cint>>(BinaryTree\u003cint>(1)),\n    new InorderBinaryTree\u003cBinaryTree\u003cint>>(BinaryTree\u003cint>(3)));\nInorderBinaryTree\u003cint> t2(2,\n    new InorderBinaryTree\u003cint>(1),\n    new InorderBinaryTree", "href": "topics/inheritance.html#exam-24b-q1"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Back to Java", "text": "The second half of the course writes servers, threads, and databases in Java. Chapter 7 is the reset: what the virtual machine actually stores, which methods are virtual, and how a failure leaves a method. The virtual machine Java is chosen here because the program runs on a virtual machine, so the same bytecode moves between operating systems, and because garbage collection removes the delete errors of the C++ half. The cost is that you have to understand the object graph the collector sees. A source file ends in .java and compiles to a .class file of bytecode, the machine language of the JVM. Bytecode is not tied to one processor. The compiler's job, as the lecture lists it, is to check the program and to compress it into that form: full type checking, access control, argument matching, checked exceptions, inheritance and interfaces, and reachability. Most of the structure survives in the class file: names, signatures, the inheritance tree, and the shape of if, while, for, and switch. The lecture's point is that bytecode is largely reversible. A program is classes only. There are no free functions and no globals. Some class has public static void main(String[] args). Where Java s", "href": "topics/java-runtime.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "int[] a = new int[10]; and String[] b = new String[10]; inside main. What is on the stack,", "text": "int[] a = new int[10]; and String[] b = new String[10]; inside main. What is on the stack, and what is on the heap?", "href": "topics/java-runtime.html#worked-array-layout"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 5 contrasts a checked exception with an unchecked one. FileNotFoundException is ch", "text": "Session 5 contrasts a checked exception with an unchecked one. FileNotFoundException is checked. ArithmeticException is unchecked. For each, say whether the program compiles if the caller ignores it, and where a thrown exception goes if no frame handles it.", "href": "topics/java-runtime.html#ps5-checked-exception"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Design by contract", "text": "A method is a deal. The caller promises a condition. The object promises that its structure still makes sense. The method promises a result. Chapter 9 names the three parts of that deal design by contract (תכנון לפי חוזה, DbC). The exams ask for the deal in words, on whatever class the question just introduced. The three clauses Preconditions, invariants, postconditions A contract between a class and its caller has three kinds of clause. A precondition (תנאי קדם) must hold at the call. If it does not, the method's behavior is not guaranteed; the lecture marks that as undefined behavior. Breaking it is the caller's bug. The condition may be stated in the vocabulary of the contract, not only in field names. An invariant (תנאי פנימי, and on exams תכונה נשמרת or אינווריאנטה) holds whenever the object is observable by a caller: at the start and at the end of every public operation. It is the consistency of the representation. Breaking it is the implementation's bug. A postcondition (תנאי סיום) holds after the operation returns. It describes the object's state and the result, independent of one caller's habits. Breaking it is the implementation's bug. The running example is Image.crop. T", "href": "topics/design-by-contract.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "State the precondition, one invariant, and the postcondition of Image.crop(x, y, w, h) as ", "text": "State the precondition, one invariant, and the postcondition of Image.crop(x, y, w, h) as the lecture frames them.", "href": "topics/design-by-contract.html#worked-crop-contract"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Using the session's rules, write the invariant of Stack, the precondition and postconditio", "text": "Using the session's rules, write the invariant of Stack, the precondition and postcondition of push, and the precondition of top. push rejects null. top returns the last pushed element and does not remove it. size is never negative, and an empty stack has size 0.", "href": "topics/design-by-contract.html#ps5-stack-contract"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2016 moed A, question 1 (30 points). Queue\u003cT> has add(T item) and remove(). a. (8) Define ", "text": "2016 moed A, question 1 (30 points). Queue\u003cT> has add(T item) and remove(). a. (8) Define an invariant for the interface, and pre- and postconditions for the methods. Add methods to the interface if you need them to state the contract. b. (6) Complete testAdd and testRemove in TestQueue. createQueue() returns the object under test. setUp builds a fresh queue. c. The question then shows the dining philosophers with a hand-rolled Semaphore and asks about deadlock, livelock, and starvation. That pa", "href": "topics/design-by-contract.html#exam-16a-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2018 moed A, question 2, Concurrency (30 points), the contract half. Stack\u003cT> is a bounded", "text": "2018 moed A, question 2, Concurrency (30 points), the contract half. Stack\u003cT> is a bounded stack. push may store null. pop removes. size is the current count. capacity is the maximum. a. (5) Define an invariant. Also state pre- and postconditions for push and pop. The rest of the question, about making the implementation safe, is the concurrency chapter.", "href": "topics/design-by-contract.html#exam-18a-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2019 moed A, question 2 (30 points). SortedBinaryTree\u003cT extends Comparable\u003cT>> has getData", "text": "2019 moed A, question 2 (30 points). SortedBinaryTree\u003cT extends Comparable\u003cT>> has getData, getLeft, getRight, and insert. a. (5) Define the invariant. The implementation keeps data, left, and right in volatile fields, rejects null with an exception, and inserts by comparing with data. Later parts ask whether that implementation is thread-safe. Answer the invariant here as a fact about a quiet tree.", "href": "topics/design-by-contract.html#exam-19a-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2024 moed A, question 2, Concurrency (30 points). Set\u003cT> has contains and add. a. (10) Def", "text": "2024 moed A, question 2, Concurrency (30 points). Set\u003cT> has contains and add. a. (10) Define an invariant. Define a precondition and a postcondition for add. Extend the interface if you need a query to state them. The given SetImpl is a linked list with a volatile head. add walks the list and returns if equals hits, otherwise it links a new node at the end. contains is not shown and is not required. The rest of the question asks you to make add safe. State the contract first.", "href": "topics/design-by-contract.html#exam-24a-q2"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Concurrency", "text": "Clock speed stopped being the way to make a computer faster. The cheap improvement is more processors. A typical machine already runs tens or hundreds of processes at once. Inside one process, the same idea is a thread (חוט ביצוע): another stack, the same heap. Processes do not share a heap Physical memory is the RAM chips. Each cell has a physical address. Virtual memory is the lie the operating system tells a process: the address space looks private, from 0 up to a huge limit, even though many processes are alive. The lecture draws that space as pages (דפים), often 4KB, mapped by the operating system onto physical frames. Two processes that both use address 0x20000 are talking about different frames. That is why one calculator cannot read another's memory, and why cooperating programs need an explicit channel. Chapter 8 reprints the memory-model picture and then puts a page table under it. A load can miss: the page is not in RAM, and the operating system brings it in. None of that is visible in a Java variable. What is visible is the consequence: threads of one process share the heap and the statics, and they do not share stacks. A thread is a stack plus a task Thread of executio", "href": "topics/concurrency.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "Two threads call increment once on a counter that starts at 0. The body is the read, add, ", "text": "Two threads call increment once on a counter that starts at 0. The body is the read, add, write sequence. Give an interleaving whose result is 1.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#worked-counter-race"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 6 asks what cnt.increment() does if two threads call it \"at the same time\" on this", "text": "Session 6 asks what cnt.increment() does if two threads call it \"at the same time\" on this class, and what the three machine steps of the body are. public class Counter {\n    private int count;\n    public Counter() { count = 0; }\n    public void increment() { count++; }\n    public int getValue() { return count; }\n}", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#ps6-counter-race"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "The session's last slide asks whether a parallel average is safe. Each worker sums a slice", "text": "The session's last slide asks whether a parallel average is safe. Each worker sums a slice into a shared total with total += part, then the main thread joins and divides by the length. Is it correct?", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#ps6-average-safe"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 7 lists three ways a thread can wait for a condition: busy waiting, sleep-and-chec", "text": "Session 7 lists three ways a thread can wait for a condition: busy waiting, sleep-and-check, and wait/notify. Say what each costs, and which one can block without polling.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#ps7-wait-vs-spin"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Name the failure: (1) two threads each hold one lock and wait for the other's, (2) a low-p", "text": "Name the failure: (1) two threads each hold one lock and wait for the other's, (2) a low-priority thread never obtains the lock because a high-priority thread keeps taking it, (3) two threads repeatedly lock, notice the other lock is taken, unlock, and try again, forever.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#ps7-liveness-names"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "The session shows a lazy singleton: if (instance == null)\n    instance = new ClassicSingle", "text": "The session shows a lazy singleton: if (instance == null)\n    instance = new ClassicSingleton(); Why is this wrong with two threads? Why is synchronized on the whole getter correct but bad for liveness? Why does the holder class work?", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#ps8-singleton"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "A worker loops until shouldStop is true. Another thread sets the flag. The worker is insid", "text": "A worker loops until shouldStop is true. Another thread sets the flag. The worker is inside sleep. Explain why the flag is not enough, and what interrupt changes.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#ps8-interrupt"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2015 moed A, question 1 (30 points). Gear knows its center, radius, and left and right nei", "text": "2015 moed A, question 1 (30 points). Gear knows its center, radius, and left and right neighbors. move turns this gear and both neighbors, in the opposite direction. SimpleGear stores a _state angle. a. Define an invariant. b. check validates constructor arguments against that invariant. c. Is the class safe under every interleaving? If not, show one. d. After _state is updated inside move, moveLeft, and moveRight, is it safe? Show a bad interleaving or argue. e. Preconditions and postconditions", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-15a-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2015 moed B, question 1 (30 points). SimpleGear's public methods are all synchronized. Nei", "text": "2015 moed B, question 1 (30 points). SimpleGear's public methods are all synchronized. Neighbors call each other's moveLeft / moveRight, which also need the neighbor's lock. a. (7) Show a deadlock of several gears. b. (18) Remove it in three ways: narrowing the synchronization, and using a semaphore instead of synchronized, and one further way that keeps the program correct. c. (5) Which way do you adopt, and why?", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-15b-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2016 moed B, question 1 (30 points). SynchQueue implements Queue with every method synchro", "text": "2016 moed B, question 1 (30 points). SynchQueue implements Queue with every method synchronized on this. ExtIntQueue adds sum() over the integers. a. (4) Pre- and postcondition of sum. b. (6) An interleaving where the postcondition fails. c. (10) Make sum correct without synchronizing the whole walk, in the optimistic style of a versioned iterator. The method must not stay locked for the whole summation. d. (10) Mystics look at a fork while thinking, and must not look at a fork someone is eating", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-16b-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2017 moed A, question 1 (30 points). A board of N by N starts with one goblet and several ", "text": "2017 moed A, question 1 (30 points). A board of N by N starts with one goblet and several players. Each player has a Strategy that picks a Direction. A player who reaches the goblet ends the game. The question asks you to finish the launch in main and to protect the board so two players cannot occupy one cell or miss the win. Locks are required; do not rewrite the whole class.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-17a-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2017 moed B, question 1 (30 points). Players on an N by N board may move one step or lookA", "text": "2017 moed B, question 1 (30 points). Players on an N by N board may move one step or lookAhead, which returns the distance to the nearest object or to the edge. Segment can test intersection and is already implemented. a. (5) Pre- and postconditions of lookAhead. The implementation must stay correct when several player threads call move and lookAhead. Segment is given as thread-safe only if you do not mutate it; it is an immutable value.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-17b-q1"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2018 moed B, question 2 (30 points). A bounded Stack has the invariant 0 \u003c= size() \u003c= capa", "text": "2018 moed B, question 2 (30 points). A bounded Stack has the invariant 0 \u003c= size() \u003c= capacity(). push and pop may fail their preconditions in the implementation you are given. Show two threads T1 and T2, and two stacks S1 and S2, that deadlock. Each thread may call push and pop on both stacks. The methods lock the stack they belong to, and a push of a stack into the other stack locks both.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-18b-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2019 moed B, question 2 (30 points). SortedBinaryTree publishes this invariant: //@INV: ge", "text": "2019 moed B, question 2 (30 points). SortedBinaryTree publishes this invariant: //@INV: getData() != null &&\n// (getLeft() == null || getData().compareTo(getLeft().getData()) > 0) &&\n// (getRight() == null || getData().compareTo(getRight().getData()) \u003c 0) The implementation inserts by walking volatile child pointers with no lock. Show an interleaving that breaks the invariant, and give a locking scheme that preserves it. A later part asks what a readers-writer lock would change.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-19b-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2020 moed A, question 2, Concurrency (30 points). Two threads share an object and run a me", "text": "2020 moed A, question 2, Concurrency (30 points). Two threads share an object and run a method that reads a field, decides, and writes it back, with no lock. List every legal output of the program and justify each. A second part asks which outputs disappear if the method is synchronized. The shape of the program on the paper is two threads calling the same unsynchronized method on one object whose field starts at a known value, each thread printing what it observed.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-20a-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2020 moed B, question 2 (30 points). Synchronization with atomic variables. Two threads t1", "text": "2020 moed B, question 2 (30 points). Synchronization with atomic variables. Two threads t1 and t2 both call f(). Show the races if f updates a plain int, and rewrite the update with AtomicInteger so the posted invariant holds. A CAS loop is the tool the question expects when the new value depends on the old one.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-20b-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2024 moed B, question 2 (30 points). A Barrier lets a fixed set of threads proceed in phas", "text": "2024 moed B, question 2 (30 points). A Barrier lets a fixed set of threads proceed in phases. await says \"I finished this phase.\" The caller blocks until every participant has finished it, then all of them enter the next phase. Implement await so that a generation counter prevents a fast thread from sneaking into the following phase and releasing the previous one early. InterruptedException must leave the barrier usable.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-24b-q2"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2024 moed C, question 2 (30 points). Set adds iterator() and clone() to contains and add. ", "text": "2024 moed C, question 2 (30 points). Set adds iterator() and clone() to contains and add. SetImpl is a linked list. An iterator that walks next pointers races with add. Make iterator and add safe. Cloning must not publish a structure another thread is in the middle of changing.", "href": "topics/concurrency.html#exam-24c-q2"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Networking", "text": "A process cannot read another process's memory. Chapter 10 starts from that isolation, including the case of two machines, and builds the channel the rest of the course will program against: a port, a socket, and a choice between TCP and UDP. Layers The lecture uses a shortened OSI stack of four layers. Each layer offers a service to the one above it, hides its own implementation, and speaks a defined protocol. layer job unit link bits on a wire, timing, the NIC and its driver frame internet addresses and routing packet transport process to process segment or datagram application what the message means message The link layer does not know who the application is. The internet layer, IP, puts a source and a destination on each packet. The header carries lengths, an identification, flags, and a TTL (time to live) that routers decrement so a packet cannot loop forever. A router looks up the next hop in a local table and hands the packet to the link layer. IP does not promise that the packet arrives, or that it arrives in order. DNS turns a name into an IP address. NAT lets a private network share one public address. The course then ignores the bottom and programs the top two layers. Ho", "href": "topics/networking.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "A TCP server has called accept once and is blocked inside read on that socket. A second cl", "text": "A TCP server has called accept once and is blocked inside read on that socket. A second client connects. What happens, and what changes if the server loops back to accept?", "href": "topics/networking.html#worked-tcp-accept"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "From session 9's comparison: for a spell-check request of one word and one response, give ", "text": "From session 9's comparison: for a spell-check request of one word and one response, give one reason to choose UDP and one reason to choose TCP.", "href": "topics/networking.html#ps9-tcp-udp"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "The session shows a Hebrew letter whose UTF-8 encoding is the bytes D7 90. The server writ", "text": "The session shows a Hebrew letter whose UTF-8 encoding is the bytes D7 90. The server writes those bytes. The client builds an InputStreamReader with the platform charset, which is not UTF-8. What does the client fail to do, and what constructor fixes it?", "href": "topics/networking.html#ps9-encoding"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Translate a Java synchronized (mutex) block that prints a task id into the C++ of session ", "text": "Translate a Java synchronized (mutex) block that prints a task id into the C++ of session 10. Include the thread launch and the wait. State the rule the session gives for destroying a std::thread.", "href": "topics/networking.html#ps10-lock-guard"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "A client wants to receive messages for /topic/a. List the STOMP frames it sends, in order,", "text": "A client wants to receive messages for /topic/a. List the STOMP frames it sends, in order, and the frame it receives if the server cannot subscribe it. Say what ends a frame.", "href": "topics/networking.html#ps10-stomp"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2015 moed B, question 3 (30 points). The messaging server from the appendix is a thread pe", "text": "2015 moed B, question 3 (30 points). The messaging server from the appendix is a thread per accepted socket. a. (5) Run at most 10 threads. b. (5) MessageLengthBasedTokenizer frames a message as a length followed by that many bytes, not as a delimiter. c. (5) SpellMessageEncoder turns a SpellMessage into bytes and back. d. (8) A spell server uses SpellMessage, the length tokenizer, and a thread-safe LexiconBasedSpellChecker with an empty constructor. e. Update SpellChecker so the server in the q", "href": "topics/networking.html#exam-15b-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2016 moed A, question 3 (30 points). A printer service was an RMI interface Printer.print(", "text": "2016 moed A, question 3 (30 points). A printer service was an RMI interface Printer.print(String). A student replaced the generated stub and skeleton with a protocol server. a. (6) At most ten handler threads. b. (6) Frame by a length, not by a delimiter. The length fits in a Java int. c. (6) Speak UDP instead of TCP. d. (6) Write a stub and a skeleton, in the same style, for Math with the methods on the exam. e. (6) Serve the printer's clients with the reactor instead of a thread per client.", "href": "topics/networking.html#exam-16a-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2017 moed B, question 3 (30 points). A bank server uses the command-invocation protocol fr", "text": "2017 moed B, question 3 (30 points). A bank server uses the command-invocation protocol from class. Three commands arrive as lines: LOGIN name (server replies ack and remembers the client), DEPOSIT money (add to that client's balance, reply ack), and a third command the paper uses to read the balance. Implement the commands and the server main that hosts them. Points on the exam: commands, then the server main.", "href": "topics/networking.html#exam-17b-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2020 moed B, question 3 (30 points). Stores maps a store name to product prices. Commands ", "text": "2020 moed B, question 3 (30 points). Stores maps a store name to product prices. Commands arrive through the command-invocation protocol and run on one shared Stores object inside a reactor on port 7777. a. Implement GetProductPrices, which takes a product name and returns the map of store name to price. b. Wire PriceServer.main so the reactor executes those commands on the one StoresImpl.", "href": "topics/networking.html#exam-20b-q3"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "The reactor", "text": "A thread per client spends a stack on each connection and blocks inside read for most of its life. A single-threaded server accepts one client and ignores the others until that conversation ends. The reactor (ריאקטור) is the third shape: one thread waits for many channels, and a small pool runs the work that is actually slow. Three servers, then a fourth The lecture's service returns a BufferedImage for a file name. The client writes the name and a newline and reads the bytes back. A single-threaded server calls accept, handles that socket to the end, and only then loops. One client at a time. A thread-per-client server calls accept and new Thread(() -> handle(s)).start(). Connections overlap. Threads are expensive, most of them are blocked in read, and nothing stops a million clients from creating a million threads. The lecture's row for this design: uncontrolled, collapses under load, wasteful, not scalable. A fixed pool, Executors.newFixedThreadPool(poolSize), caps the count. Extra connections wait in the queue. The threads are still blocked in I/O, so a pool of eight stuck in read serves nobody else. Reactor An event-driven server does not give a thread to each client. One thre", "href": "topics/reactor.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "A ByteBuffer of capacity 16 has just had 5 bytes read into it. Position is 5, limit is 16.", "text": "A ByteBuffer of capacity 16 has just had 5 bytes read into it. Position is 5, limit is 16. You want to write those 5 bytes to a channel. What does flip change?", "href": "topics/reactor.html#worked-flip"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 10's buffer diagram labels position, limit, and capacity, then shows Write, Read, ", "text": "Session 10's buffer diagram labels position, limit, and capacity, then shows Write, Read, and Flip. Starting from a cleared buffer of capacity 8, you put three bytes. Give position and limit before flip and after flip. Then say which range a write to a channel will send.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#ps10-flip"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "A client sends message M1 and then M2. The server creates tasks R1 and R2. Why can a norma", "text": "A client sends message M1 and then M2. The server creates tasks R1 and R2. Why can a normal thread pool run R2 before R1, and what does the actor pool change?", "href": "topics/reactor.html#ps11-actor-order"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "The lecture lists four mistakes. Pick the one that burns a core while no client is sending", "text": "The lecture lists four mistakes. Pick the one that burns a core while no client is sending, and say the interestOps change that stops it.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#ps11-write-interest"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2015 moed A, question 3 (30 points). Keep the reactor, but talk to every client through on", "text": "2015 moed A, question 3 (30 points). Keep the reactor, but talk to every client through one DatagramSocketChannel instead of a TCP SocketChannel per client. send and receive are non-blocking and return immediately. receive returns the sender's address, or null if nothing arrived. a. (20) Say which methods you change. Do not rewrite methods you leave alone. b. Name one advantage and one disadvantage of UDP here. Can one DatagramSocketChannel replace the per-client channels? Does a channel per cli", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-15a-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2016 moed B, question 3 (30 points). The reactor's executor picks a task with no regard to", "text": "2016 moed B, question 3 (30 points). The reactor's executor picks a task with no regard to how many bytes that client has already transferred, and no regard to how many requests that client has already had served. A busy client can be chosen forever. a. (10) Give one short scenario for each unfairness. b. (20) Implement two policies: prefer the client with the fewest bytes so far, and prefer the client with the fewest requests so far. You may introduce a PriorityBlockingQueue whose order is comp", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-16b-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2017 moed A, question 3 (30 points). Today the selector thread reads a ByteBuffer and a po", "text": "2017 moed A, question 3 (30 points). Today the selector thread reads a ByteBuffer and a pool thread tokenizes and runs the message. A student wants the selector thread to tokenize as well, and to submit one Runnable per message. a. (10) Apply that change. b. (10) Show a run where the change hurts. c. (10) Implement ioThroughput and compThroughput for the two stages.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-17a-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2018 moed A, question 3 (30 points). The selector thread is saturated. a. Shraga says: add", "text": "2018 moed A, question 3 (30 points). The selector thread is saturated. a. Shraga says: add workers to the actor pool, and move the server to a machine with more processors, to take load off the selector. Does that work? b. Nahum's team instead adds a feature to the reactor. Argue the selector-side fix.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-18a-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2018 moed B, question 3 (30 points). Shraga benchmarks a thread-per-client server and a re", "text": "2018 moed B, question 3 (30 points). Shraga benchmarks a thread-per-client server and a reactor by sending a few requests from his laptop. The thread-per-client server answers faster, so he tells the company to switch. Nahum says the conclusion is wrong. Explain Nahum, including what the benchmark failed to stress.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-18b-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2019 moed A, question 3 (25 points). The actor pool promises two things for each connectio", "text": "2019 moed A, question 3 (25 points). The actor pool promises two things for each connection: at most one request runs at a time, and if request 1 arrived before request 2 then response 1 is sent before response 2. a. (8) Explain how the pool does that, naming the queue. b. A change that hurts performance. c. A change you would actually want.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-19a-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2019 moed B, question 3 (25 points). Change execute and complete so that when a worker fin", "text": "2019 moed B, question 3 (25 points). Change execute and complete so that when a worker finishes a request it runs the next waiting request of the same client on the same worker, with no new Runnable, until that client's queue is empty. Keep the reactor's split: the selector does I/O, workers do protocol.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-19b-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2020 moed A, question 3 (30 points). Add logging of server actions to the reactor. Logger ", "text": "2020 moed A, question 3 (30 points). Add logging of server actions to the reactor. Logger wraps an OutputStream. Say where each log call goes so the selector thread does not block on disk, and so two workers do not tear a log line.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-20a-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2024 moed A, question 3, Communication and databases (35 points). A student changes Reacto", "text": "2024 moed A, question 3, Communication and databases (35 points). A student changes Reactor.handleReadWrite to hand the ready key to Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2) named communicationThreads. Explain why two communication threads calling handleReadWrite on the same selector's keys is unsafe, and what work, if any, may move to that pool. The question continues into a database; keep the selector correct first.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-24a-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2024 moed B, question 3 (35 points). A logistics warehouse stores volunteers, evacuated fa", "text": "2024 moed B, question 3 (35 points). A logistics warehouse stores volunteers, evacuated families, and food portions. DB is thread-safe, has an empty constructor, and executeQuery returns the rows or throws SQLException. Build DBServer: a reactor with ten worker threads. A client sends a query string. The server runs it on the one DB and replies with the first row, or \"Result No\" when there are no rows, or the error text when the query throws. Do not construct a DB per connection.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-24b-q3"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2024 moed C, question 3 (35 points). a. (10) Change the server template so a client sends ", "text": "2024 moed C, question 3 (35 points). a. (10) Change the server template so a client sends a message of type A and receives a reply of type B. Name the spots you edit. Do not rewrite whole classes. b. Complete Command and RemoteCommandInvocationProtocol. c. DB.executeQuery returns ArrayList\u003cString> and is thread-safe. DBServer uses BaseServer with a pool of 11 and an ObjectEncoderDecoder that already exists. One DB serves every client.", "href": "topics/reactor.html#exam-24c-q3"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Serialization", "text": "The image server in the reactor spoke a private protocol: a file name in, the file's bytes out. The next server sends objects. An object is not a byte array until something walks its fields and writes them down. That walk is serialization (סיריאליזציה). What is written Serialization Serialization is a representation of an object as a sequence of bytes. It includes the object's type, the shape of its fields, and the field values. Deserialization rebuilds an object from that sequence. The pair stores primitive fields and object fields. It can move a value between machines and between JVMs, and the bytes can travel in a file or on a socket. What it does not store is the identity of the original heap object. The result of reading the bytes is a new object with the same field values. Serializable is a marker interface (ממשק סימון). It has no methods. It is a flag the JVM checks. ObjectOutputStream and ObjectInputStream refuse an object whose class does not implement it. public final class Pet implements Serializable {\n    public enum Type { DOG, CAT }\n    public final String name;\n    public final Type type;\n    public final int age;\n    public Pet(String name, Type type, int age) {\n   ", "href": "topics/serialization.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "Pet is serialized with name == \"A\" and a cached hash that was already computed. The field ", "text": "Pet is serialized with name == \"A\" and a cached hash that was already computed. The field is transient. What does hash contain in the object readObject returns, and what does name contain?", "href": "topics/serialization.html#worked-transient-hash"}, {"kind": "Topic", "title": "Persistence and SQL", "text": "RAM forgets. It needs power, it is wiped on reboot, it dies with the process, and it is the expensive, smaller store. A disk keeps bytes until somebody deletes them, and it is large. Serialization can put an object on that disk. Chapter 13 lists why that is not yet a database: a lookup deserializes whole objects, there is no index, an update rewrites the file, concurrency is a lock you invent, and there are no transactions. A database management system is the runtime for that data. The lecture names MS SQL Server, MySQL, Oracle, and SQLite. Underneath, the layers are disk access, file access, operations on rows, and queries. The relational model Relational database A relational database (בסיס נתונים רלציוני) stores rows in tables. A column has a name and a type. A primary key (מפתח ראשי) identifies one row. A foreign key (מפתח זר) is a reference to a key in another table, and it should be unique at the target, usually that table's primary key. A column may be NOT NULL or UNIQUE. The design rule on the slides: a class becomes a table, a reference becomes a foreign key, and a list becomes another table. The pair (student id, course id) in that extra table is the primary key of the li", "href": "topics/persistence.html"}, {"kind": "Example", "title": "Alice is enrolled in prog. Bob is enrolled in nothing. prog and systems both exist. What d", "text": "Alice is enrolled in prog. Bob is enrolled in nothing. prog and systems both exist. What does the inner join of students to courses through StudentsCourses return, and what does a left join add?", "href": "topics/persistence.html#worked-inner-join"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 12's PRACTICAL_SESSIONS has columns TA_ID, GroupNum, Location, Time. TEACHING_ASSI", "text": "Session 12's PRACTICAL_SESSIONS has columns TA_ID, GroupNum, Location, Time. TEACHING_ASSISTANTS has ID, Name, OfficeHours. Write the two CREATE TABLE statements. GroupNum is the primary key of a session. ID is the primary key of an assistant. A session's TA_ID refers to an assistant.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#ps12-foreign-key"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Using the tables above, write a query that returns Time, Location, GroupNum, and the assis", "text": "Using the tables above, write a query that returns Time, Location, GroupNum, and the assistant's Name for every session that has an assistant. Then say what a left join from assistants adds that this query hides. The session's sample has Shaked on two groups and Matan on none.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#ps12-join"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "Session 13's grader has tables students(id, name), assignments(num, expected_output), and ", "text": "Session 13's grader has tables students(id, name), assignments(num, expected_output), and grades(student_id, assignment_num, grade). Name the DTO, the DAO, and the repository method that returns a grade together with the student's name. Say why that last method is not on the grades DAO.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#ps13-dto-dao"}, {"kind": "Exercise", "title": "The session replaces a hand-written find with row_map(row, col_mapping, dto_type), which b", "text": "The session replaces a hand-written find with row_map(row, col_mapping, dto_type), which builds constructor arguments by column index and calls the DTO constructor. Assignment.find used to be return Assignment(c.fetchone()). What must col_mapping contain so the generic find still constructs Assignment(num, expected_output)?", "href": "topics/persistence.html#ps13-orm-find"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2015 moed A, question 4 (10 points). BigApple stores a customer (name, phone, join date, a", "text": "2015 moed A, question 4 (10 points). BigApple stores a customer (name, phone, join date, and links to other customers who are family) and each action on the account (a date and a kind: sent message, outgoing call, missed call, received message, with the other phone number). a. Define the tables. b. SQL for the messages received by members of Ringo Starr's family.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-15a-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2015 moed B, question 4 (10 points). Extend BigApple with the kind of family relation (par", "text": "2015 moed B, question 4 (10 points). Extend BigApple with the kind of family relation (parent, sibling, child), a table of competing carriers, and a history of number portability: the date the customer left, and the carrier they left to. a. (5) The extra tables. b. (5) Every carrier Ringo Starr has moved to, with the date.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-15b-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2016 moed A, question 4 (10 points). Abbey Road stores recording sessions. A session has a", "text": "2016 moed A, question 4 (10 points). Abbey Road stores recording sessions. A session has a date, a song, a band, and many takes. A take has people in roles: players (a person may play several instruments on one take), singers, and engineers. a. (5) Tables. b. (5) The take numbers and song names where Billy Preston played keyboards during the Beatles' Get Back sessions, from January 1969, ordered by date ascending.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-16a-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2016 moed B, question 4 (10 points). The Abbey Road schema from moed A is given. a. Show h", "text": "2016 moed B, question 4 (10 points). The Abbey Road schema from moed A is given. a. Show how Kim Philby can appear as a participant in the role of double agent. b. Restrict roles to a fixed set: player, singer, engineer.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-16b-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2017 moed A, question 4 (15 points). An online glasses shop. Stock a model (model number, ", "text": "2017 moed A, question 4 (15 points). An online glasses shop. Stock a model (model number, price). Register a customer (name, id). A purchase records the customer id and the model, and the shop issues a receipt with a unique number and the purchase details. A customer can ask the price of a model. The owner can list every receipt. Give the tables and the two queries: price of a model, and all receipts.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-17a-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2017 moed B, question 4 (15 points). A garage. A job has a car number, a fault description", "text": "2017 moed B, question 4 (15 points). A garage. A job has a car number, a fault description, and a job id. A worker has a name and an id. Assigning a worker to a job records the worker, the job, and that it is not finished. Finishing a job marks it done. Tables, plus the query for a worker's unfinished jobs.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-17b-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2018 moed A, question 4 (10 points). Exam questions are stored so that a question has a te", "text": "2018 moed A, question 4 (10 points). Exam questions are stored so that a question has a text, and a record of the years it was used as moed A and as moed B. A question may repeat in different years and must not be used twice in the same year. Choose the schema that enforces that, among the CREATE TABLE options on the paper, and say which key does the work.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-18a-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2018 moed B, question 4 (10 points). Police wiretaps. A recording stores the text, the cal", "text": "2018 moed B, question 4 (10 points). Police wiretaps. A recording stores the text, the caller's number, the callee's number, and the date. Each phone number has an owner name and address. The paper starts: CREATE TABLE phone (\n  phone_num INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  address TEXT NOT NULL\n); Finish the schema and write a query for the recordings of a given number, with both owners' names.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-18b-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2019 moed A, question 4 (15 points). Airbnb. A user has an id and may be a guest, a host, ", "text": "2019 moed A, question 4 (15 points). Airbnb. A user has an id and may be a guest, a host, or both. A property has an id, one host, a nightly price, and a description. A rental has a guest, a property (and therefore that property's host), a check-in, and a check-out. After checkout, the host writes a recommendation of the guest and the guest writes one of the host. Three parts on the paper ask you to complete the schema and one query. Give the tables.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-19a-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2019 moed B, question 4 (15 points). The same Airbnb facts, plus: two guests cannot share ", "text": "2019 moed B, question 4 (15 points). The same Airbnb facts, plus: two guests cannot share a property on the same night. Enforce that in the schema, not in a comment.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-19b-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2020 moed A, question 4 (15 points). A DVD service. Users have an id and a name. Movies ha", "text": "2020 moed A, question 4 (15 points). A DVD service. Users have an id and a name. Movies have an id, a name, a description, any number of categories from {Action, Drama, Romance, kids}, an average score, and num_scorers. A watch records the user, the movie, and the date. Tables, and a query for the movies a user watched, with their categories.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-20a-q4"}, {"kind": "Exam question", "title": "2020 moed B, question 4 (15 points). The same DVD service as moed A. Write the query that ", "text": "2020 moed B, question 4 (15 points). The same DVD service as moed A. Write the query that updates a movie's average when a user submits a new score s, using the stored score and num_scorers, and the query that lists movies in a category ordered by score.", "href": "topics/persistence.html#exam-20b-q4"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "stack", "text": "The LIFO region of automatic storage, one activation frame per call, freed when the call returns.", "href": "glossary.html#stack"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "heap", "text": "The region of dynamic storage obtained with new and released only by a matching delete.", "href": "glossary.html#heap"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "pointer", "text": "An object whose value is the address of another object, or null.", "href": "glossary.html#pointer"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "reference", "text": "A name bound to an existing object. It cannot be null and it cannot be reseated.", "href": "glossary.html#reference"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "destructor", "text": "The special member function that runs when an object's lifetime ends and that frees what the object owns.", "href": "glossary.html#destructor"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "copy constructor", "text": "The constructor that builds a new object as a deep copy of another of the same type.", "href": "glossary.html#copy-constructor"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "assignment operator", "text": "The operator that replaces an existing object's resources with a copy of another's, and that must stay safe if the two are the same object.", "href": "glossary.html#assignment-operator"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "move constructor", "text": "The constructor that steals a source object's buffer and leaves that source empty so its destructor frees nothing.", "href": "glossary.html#move-constructor"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "RAII", "text": "Binding a resource's lifetime to an object's lifetime, so the destructor is the release.", "href": "glossary.html#raii"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "unique_ptr", "text": "A smart pointer that owns one object, cannot be copied, and can be moved.", "href": "glossary.html#unique_ptr"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "shared_ptr", "text": "A smart pointer that shares ownership through a count and deletes the object when the count hits zero.", "href": "glossary.html#shared_ptr"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "virtual function", "text": "A member function whose implementation is chosen from the dynamic type of the object, through the vtable.", "href": "glossary.html#virtual-function"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "vtable", "text": "The per-class table of virtual-function addresses. Each object holds a pointer to its class's table.", "href": "glossary.html#vtable"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "invariant", "text": "A condition that is true of an object whenever no method of the object is running.", "href": "glossary.html#invariant"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "precondition", "text": "What the caller must make true before a method runs. The method may assume it.", "href": "glossary.html#precondition"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "postcondition", "text": "What the method guarantees when it returns, provided the precondition held.", "href": "glossary.html#postcondition"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "thread", "text": "A sequential flow of execution that shares the process heap with the other threads.", "href": "glossary.html#thread"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "deadlock", "text": "A wait cycle in which each thread holds a lock the next thread needs.", "href": "glossary.html#deadlock"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "livelock", "text": "Threads keep changing state in response to each other and never make progress.", "href": "glossary.html#livelock"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "starvation", "text": "A thread that is ready to run is passed over indefinitely by the scheduler or by the lock.", "href": "glossary.html#starvation"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "TCP", "text": "A connection-oriented, reliable byte stream. A read returns some of the bytes, not necessarily one message.", "href": "glossary.html#tcp"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "UDP", "text": "A connectionless datagram protocol. There is no accept, and a datagram arrives whole or not at all.", "href": "glossary.html#udp"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "reactor", "text": "One thread that waits in select and dispatches ready non-blocking channels.", "href": "glossary.html#reactor"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "selector", "text": "The Java NIO object that reports which registered channels are ready to read or write.", "href": "glossary.html#selector"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "serialization", "text": "A representation of an object as a byte sequence that records its type, the shape of its fields, and the field values.", "href": "glossary.html#serialization"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "primary key", "text": "The column or columns that identify one row of a table.", "href": "glossary.html#primary-key"}, {"kind": "Glossary", "title": "foreign key", "text": "A column whose values must equal a key of another table, the relational form of a pointer.", "href": "glossary.html#foreign-key"}];
